ty and uh law enforcement  agencies  um but we have to be vigilant because um  these people are trained to use  explosives  and weapons they are indoctrinated  by the salafi ideology they have  developed a network  around the mediterranean in the arab  world and  more more problematic in a way they have  raised significantly the level of  tolerance to violence  so um what do we do it it requires  member states to  assess each and every case each and  every returnee  to assess the dangerousness of the  person  then not all of them will not come back  in mount attack of course  many will need psychological support  because they will have this  post-traumatic stress  some will need just uh social support to  get  to know their life to get back to normal  life some will have to be  sent to a criminal court because they  have been part of a terrorist  organization they have committed war  crime or crime against humanity or just  terrorist crime  and where we don't have the evidence  because that's one of the challenge we  are faced with  because we're not present in syria we  will have and we assess that they are  dangerous  we will have to monitor them  the the eu as a as a group of 28 member  states has decided to react very quickly  in june 2013 ministers of interior have  adopted a quite comprehensive strategy  with more than 22 measures and we're  currently implementing  and it's around the following main  access first we have to understand  better the phenomenon so that requires  that member states share  all the information they have we have to  prevent people from being radicalized  and go to syria  and that requires a lot of work at local  level we have to detect suspicious  travel  and that requires the use of several  mechanisms we have to maximize  a database like the schengen information  system we have to set up  a new passenger name record we're  currently discussing that with japan  parliament  there is a need to deal with returnees i  just mentioned  how we should do the criminal law  aspect is important there is no current  debate as to whether the eu should not  have a common  definition of the behavior it's no  required by  the u.n security council resolution  adopted  last week under the chairmanship of  president obama  and finally we have to engage more with  third countries we have to help  especially  the country on the other side of the  military in north africa  because they too are confronted with the  phenomenon  ministers will first assess the  implementation of the  the package of measures that were  decided in june 2013  and explore further measures uh the  european council  in late august asked that the strategy  be implemented  more quickly and uh that we  explore possible new uh measures if need  be  so the main discussion will be on how to  secure this agreement with european  parliament on the setting up of an  uh european union passenger name record  uh the european council  would like it to be adopted by the end  of 2014  so we don't have much time to convince  the european parliament  and the second one is a more political  question  which is how do can we improve  the check at external borders  in two ways first check on on travel  documents  and there are ways to be more systematic  to use more  the interpol lost and stolen travel  document database  and the other one is a more sensitive  but political one i think very important  is shouldn't we contemplate a more a  systematic check  of eu citizens against the database so  if someone  is thought for arrest because he has  committed a murder  or someone is thought for arrest because  he went to syria and we know that it's  to join  the isil the terrorist organization or  jabata nusra  a systematic check would allow the  police to  arrest it at the border when they leave  and when they return so these are two  questions but there are  other technical aspects that minister  will discuss in order to boost the work  and just do what the european council  request them to do to be  to to work more quickly  you

